// marginsheet-sync. Top level is dev; staging and production are named envs.
//
// THE DEPLOYABLE WITH NO PUBLIC ROUTES. M4 section 2a ruled a third Worker so
// the Plaid token's decryption key lives somewhere no household request can
// reach. There is deliberately no "routes" and no custom domain in any
// environment: reachable only over a service binding from api, and by Cron and
// Queues later. A route added here would undo the ruling, which is why its
// absence is asserted by a test rather than left to review.
//
// It authenticates as marginsheet_sync, narrowed in migration 0023 to nine
// tables. Its credential comes from scripts/sync-db-url.mts, which refuses to
// mint one for a role wider than that.
//
// DURABLE OBJECT MIGRATIONS, DEV AND STAGING ONLY. v2 DELETES HouseholdSync.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS: a hand-deploy left durable state main cannot satisfy. The
// chain lock was deployed to dev and staging from a feature branch on 19 Aug
// 2026, which registered a HouseholdSync namespace on both. A DO NAMESPACE
// OUTLIVES THE DEPLOY THAT MADE IT, so every later deploy from main was
// refused: "New version of script does not export class 'HouseholdSync' which
// is depended on by existing Durable Objects" [code: 10064]. A redeploy cannot
// undo that; only a migration can.
//
// WHY PRODUCTION HAS NONE, AND THE ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT: production was
// never hand-deployed, so it has no such namespace, and whether Cloudflare
// treats deleting a never-created class as a no-op or an error is NOT KNOWN
// HERE. Applying it uniformly would be guessing on the one environment that
// cannot be tested first, to fix a problem it does not have. The migration is
// scoped to the environments where the namespace is known to exist.
//
// Deleting is correct: nothing ever wrote to those objects and they hold no
// household data. The branch introducing the class for real re-adds it under a
// LATER TAG per environment, never by editing this one. Tags are applied once
// and recorded, so editing a tag after it has run is the append-only rule
// wearing different clothes.
//
// WHY v2 AND NOT v1, WHICH THIS FILE GOT WRONG ONCE. The hand-deploy applied
// v1 (new_sqlite_classes) to dev and staging, so v1 is already RECORDED there.
// A delete written as v1 is skipped silently: the deploy still failed with the
// same 10064, and nothing said the migration had been ignored. The paragraph
// above already said tags are applied once, and it was written in the same
// change that reused one. PROBED, NOT REASONED: the second deploy attempt is
// what said so.
//
// CONSEQUENCE FOR THE BRANCH THAT RE-ADDS THE CLASS: dev and staging will have
// v1 and v2 recorded, so the class comes back as v3 there, while production
// has nothing recorded and takes v1. Divergent tag lists per environment are
// legal and are the honest state; making them uniform would mean claiming a
// migration ran somewhere it did not.
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "marginsheet-sync-dev",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "migrations": [{ "tag": "v2", "deleted_classes": ["HouseholdSync"] }],
  "vars": {
    "ENVIRONMENT": "dev",
    "SENTRY_DSN": "https://30ce4a490e0cad1da24789eee4c5471a@o4511912456617984.ingest.us.sentry.io/4511912470315008"
  },
  "env": {
    "staging": {
      "name": "marginsheet-sync-staging",
      "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
      "migrations": [{ "tag": "v2", "deleted_classes": ["HouseholdSync"] }],
      "vars": {
        "ENVIRONMENT": "staging",
        "SENTRY_DSN": "https://30ce4a490e0cad1da24789eee4c5471a@o4511912456617984.ingest.us.sentry.io/4511912470315008"
      }
    },
    "production": {
      "name": "marginsheet-sync",
      "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
      "vars": {
        "ENVIRONMENT": "production",
        "SENTRY_DSN": "https://30ce4a490e0cad1da24789eee4c5471a@o4511912456617984.ingest.us.sentry.io/4511912470315008"
      }
    }
  }
}
